<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">death</ansi>

When your hitpoints fall to zero, you die at once. There is no downed or
bleeding-out stage, so nobody can save you after the blow lands. Any spell
you were casting is lost, and every condition on you ends, good or bad.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Death Penalties:</ansi>
  - One of your core attributes, picked at random, may permanently weaken
    slightly.
  - One of your skills, picked at random, may grow rusty and lose a little.
    Any skill can be picked, even one you use every day.
  - You wake at your home point with your health, stamina and conviction
    nearly empty. Rest and recover before you go back out.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">A Word on Early Forgiveness:</ansi>
  Only your first few lessons are protected. Until you have trained a
  handful of skill ranks, death takes no attributes and no skills. That
  point comes quickly, often within your first few fights, and the game
  does not warn you when the grace runs out. Learn to <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> now,
  while the lesson is cheap.

The best defense against death is staying aware of your health and knowing
when to <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Submission Exception:</ansi>
  If you were knocked out by a grapple submission (subdue or
  cripple outcome), you wake at the temple with NO attribute or
  skill loss, because your opponent chose not to kill you. A
  cripple submission also leaves a broken limb that persists for
  around an hour of play time before recovering naturally.
  A portion of your carried gold is transferred to your opponent
  in either case.
  See <ansi fg="command">help submission</ansi>.

<ansi fg="magenta-bold">See also:</ansi> <ansi fg="command">help sethome</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="command">help submission</ansi>
